// Pull request author: Gideon145
// Change: new rule
// Title: Nsenter Escape to Host Namespace
// Sigma ID: b48ed9ae-609f-4c0f-a1ac-c687aa1ee11b
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Gideon Opukeme
// Date: 2026-10-18 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1611
// Description: Detects the use of nsenter to enter the host process and mount namespaces from within a
//   container. This is a common container escape technique used to break out of a container and
//   execute commands on the underlying host.
// False positives:
//   - Administrators using nsenter to debug or manage containers from the host
// References:
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1611/T1611.md
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/258ad25218376be68f6342d7ce6c29a99f5f020a/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_nsenter_container_escape.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "/nsenter" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "--mount=/proc/1/ns/" or ProcessCommandLine contains "--net=/proc/1/ns/" or ProcessCommandLine contains "-t 1" or ProcessCommandLine contains "--target 1")
